## Solution:
The error occurs because your local branch and the remote branch have diverged, and Git needs to know how to handle the differences. You can resolve this by choosing one of the following commands based on your preference:

1. Merge (Default Behavior)
If you want to merge the changes from the remote branch into your local branch, run:
git config pull.rebase false
git pull
This will create a merge commit to combine the changes.
2. Rebase
If you want to reapply your local changes on top of the remote branch (linear history), run:
git config pull.rebase true
git pull
Note: This rewrites the commit history to avoid merge commits.
3. Fast-Forward Only
If you want to pull only if the remote branch is ahead of your local branch (no divergent changes), run:
git config pull.ff only
git pull
This will fail if there are conflicting changes between the branches.
4. Set Global Default
If you want to set a default behavior for all repositories, add the --global flag. For example:

To always merge:
git config --global pull.rebase false
To always rebase:
git config --global pull.rebase true
To always fast-forward:
git config --global pull.ff only
Recommendation:
If you're unsure, start with the merge option (git config pull.rebase false) as it is the safest and most common approach.

