## I got errors, how to fix it?
1. Force Pull to Merge Unrelated Histories
Run the following command to allow merging unrelated histories:
git pull origin main --allow-unrelated-histories

Note: This will merge the remote repository's history with your local repository. You may be prompted to resolve merge conflicts if there are any differences between the two repositories.
2. Resolve Merge Conflicts (If Any)
If there are merge conflicts, Git will pause and ask you to resolve them. Open the conflicting files in your editor (e.g., Visual Studio Code), resolve the conflicts, and then stage the resolved files:
git add <file_with_conflict>

After resolving all conflicts, commit the changes:
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflicts"

3. Push the Changes to the Remote Repository
Once the histories are merged, push your local branch to the remote repository:
git push origin main

4. Set Upstream Tracking (Optional)
To avoid specifying the remote and branch every time you pull or push, set the upstream branch:
git branch --set-upstream-to=origin/main main
Note: Now you can simply use git pull or git push without specifying the remote and branch.

Summary of Commands:
git pull origin main --allow-unrelated-histories
# Resolve conflicts if prompted
git add <file_with_conflict>
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflicts"
git push origin main
git branch --set-upstream-to=origin/main main
Note: After completing these steps, your local branch will be successfully pushed to the remote repository, and the histories will be synchronized.
